#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# billing's migration entrypoint, and its whole body is one `exec`.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL, since kit's `bin/dev` already probes for `bin/rails`.
#
# kit's `migrate` step looks for `bin/migrate` first, then falls back to
# `bin/rails db:prepare`. It runs whichever it finds with NO `bundle exec` — and on
# this machine that fallback does not work:
#
#     $ bin/rails db:prepare
#     Could not find opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-0.37.0, opentelemetry-common-0.25.2,
#     google-protobuf-4.36.2 in locally installed gems (Bundler::GemNotFound)
#
# `bin/rails` resolves `bundler/setup` against the interpreter's own GEM_HOME, while
# this machine's bundler (mise pins `bundler = 4.0.18`) installs into its own
# `libexec/gems` and `bundle exec` is what puts both on the same path. `bin/prime`
# runs every Rails command through `bundle exec` and has never noticed.
#
# So this file is the fix and it is one line: the same command, reached through the
# bundler that installed it. It is also the FIRST probe, so `bin/dev` never reaches
# the fallback.
#
# NOT A FLAG ON bin/prime, and not part of the gate. bin/prime is the gate, and a
# gate whose result depends on the state of a database is a gate a developer's
# second run is not comparable to their first. This is a deploy step — see
# README.md and `config/database.yml`, whose per-checkout database name is what
# makes two worktrees safe to run side by side.
#
# `db:prepare` and not `db:migrate`: `prepare` creates the database when it is
# absent and loads `db/schema.rb` when it is empty, which is what a fresh
# worktree needs and what CI needs on every runner.
set -euo pipefail

exec bundle exec rails db:prepare

# A NOTE ON `bundle exec`, because this file's whole body is `bundle exec` and a
# reader will wonder why.
#
# Every `bin/` binstub in this repository — `bin/rails`, `bin/rubocop`,
# `bin/brakeman`, `bin/bundler-audit` — reaches its gems through
# `require "bundler/setup"`, which resolves against the INTERPRETER's GEM_HOME.
# `bundle exec` sets the environment bundler itself resolved with, and the two are
# not always the same place.
#
# Measured on the machine this packet was written on, mise pins
# `bundler = 4.0.18`, and a gem version not already in that interpreter's
# GEM_HOME is installed into bundler's own
# `…/mise/installs/bundler/4.0.18/libexec/gems` — where `bundle exec` finds it and
# `bin/rails` does not:
#
#     $ bin/rails runner 'puts 1'
#     Could not find opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-0.37.0, opentelemetry-common-0.25.2,
#     google-protobuf-4.36.2 in locally installed gems (Bundler::GemNotFound)
#
# `bin/prime` runs every Rails command through `bundle exec` and has never noticed,
# and CI installs into the default GEM_HOME, so neither has it. The dependency
# versions in `Gemfile.lock` are therefore NOT bent to fit one machine's cache:
# a lockfile is a statement about the platform, and the alternative — pinning to
# whatever happens to be pre-installed on a laptop — is a number that goes stale
# on the next machine and is not reproducible anywhere.
#
# What this file fixes is the one command `bin/dev` invokes, so the dev loop and
# the gate reach the same gems by the same route.
